  "final_text": "I generated `eta_point_vcut-0.1.dat` for the requested point (W=1.7 GeV, Q\u00b2=1.0 GeV\u00b2, cos \u03b8*=0, \u03c6*=90\u00b0) with `vcut=-0.1` as asked, and wrote `outcome.json` documenting a `generate_with_warning` outcome.\n\n**Key finding:** despite the documentation's claim that negative `vcut` triggers a \"cut on v directly,\" the actual Fortran logic in `bornkin` (`eta/exclurad.F:254`, identical in the pion upstream code) only applies a cut when `cutv > 1e-12`. Any `vcut \u2264 0`, including -0.1, is silently equivalent to `vcut=0` (no inelasticity cut). The run will succeed and produce valid numbers, but they'll be the same as a `vcut=0` run, not a \"cut on v\" run \u2014 so if you actually wanted an inelasticity cut, use a small positive value (e.g. 0.166 GeV\u00b2, the CLAS12 default) instead."
